WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.515
The Gift of the Magi

00:00:02.165 --> 00:00:04.277
One dollar and eighty-seven cents.

00:00:04.527 --> 00:00:05.465
That was all.

00:00:05.715 --> 00:00:13.203
And sixty cents of it was in copper pennies, saved one and two at a time whenever Della went to the grocer and the market.

00:00:13.453 --> 00:00:20.728
Three times Della counted the little stack of coins on the parlor table, and it was still one dollar and eighty-seven cents.

00:00:21.578 --> 00:00:23.519
The very next day would be Christmas.

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:30.276
Della sat quietly on the little floral settee in their upstairs parlor and sighed a soft, wistful sigh.

00:00:30.526 --> 00:00:37.075
She loved her husband Jim with all her heart, and she wanted so much to buy him a wonderful Christmas present.

00:00:37.925 --> 00:00:42.640
Della and Jim lived in a cozy, humble flat high up in an old brick house.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:49.695
Downstairs by the front door stood a tiny brass letter-box and a polished nameplate for James Dillingham Young.

00:00:49.945 --> 00:00:55.044
They did not have much money anymore, for Jim’s wages had grown smaller that winter.

00:00:55.294 --> 00:01:01.758
Yet every evening when Jim came up the stairs, Della met him at the door with the biggest, warmest hug in the world.

00:01:02.608 --> 00:01:09.989
Della stood by the frosty parlor window and watched a fluffy grey cat walk along a snowy wooden fence in the yard below.

00:01:10.239 --> 00:01:19.605
For weeks she had dreamed of finding a gift for Jim that was fine, rare, and sterling — something truly worthy of her kind and hardworking husband.

00:01:19.855 --> 00:01:23.887
How could she ever find it for only one dollar and eighty-seven cents?

00:01:24.737 --> 00:01:29.878
Now, in Della and Jim’s house there were two treasures in which they both took a mighty pride.

00:01:30.128 --> 00:01:36.848
One was Jim’s shining gold pocket watch, which had belonged to his father and to his grandfather before him.

00:01:37.098 --> 00:01:45.439
The other was Della’s own glorious chestnut hair, which rippled and shone like a cascade of brown waters all the way past her waist.

00:01:46.289 --> 00:01:52.497
All at once Della turned from the window and stood before the tall, narrow looking-glass between the windows.

00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:55.158
Her eyes shone with a sudden, bright idea!

00:01:55.408 --> 00:02:01.189
For one quiet moment she looked at her long, shimmering hair, and then she coiled it neatly back up.

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:08.991
She buttoned on her warm old brown winter coat, tied on her little brown hat, and hurried down the stairs into the snowy street.

00:02:09.841 --> 00:02:17.543
Down the street Della hurried until she reached the neat shop of Madame Sofronie, who bought and sold fine hair goods of all kinds.

00:02:17.793 --> 00:02:24.811
Della stepped up to the wooden counter, took off her hat, and asked if Madame Sofronie would buy her long brown hair.

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:30.309
“Twenty dollars,” said Madame Sofronie kindly, admiring the rich brown tresses.

00:02:30.559 --> 00:02:33.567
— “Give it to me quick!” said Della with a brave smile.

00:02:34.417 --> 00:02:42.588
Now Della had twenty-one dollars and eighty-seven cents in her purse, and her hair was cropped short in soft little curls beneath her hat.

00:02:42.838 --> 00:02:45.761
The next two hours flew by as if on rosy wings!

00:02:46.011 --> 00:02:54.757
She went from shop to shop along the snowy avenues, peering into glowing Christmas windows to find the very best gift in the city for Jim.

00:02:55.607 --> 00:02:58.210
At last she found it in a quiet jeweler’s shop.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:06.161
It was a braided platinum fob chain for a pocket watch — simple, strong, and shining, with no gaudy ornaments at all.

00:03:06.411 --> 00:03:09.099
It was quiet and genuine, just like Jim.

00:03:09.349 --> 00:03:13.573
As soon as Della saw it, she knew it belonged on Jim’s grand gold watch.

00:03:13.823 --> 00:03:19.626
She paid twenty-one dollars for the chain and hurried home with eighty-seven cents left in her pocket.

00:03:20.476 --> 00:03:29.500
When Della reached home, she brushed and arranged her short hair before the looking-glass until her whole head was covered with tiny, soft ringlet curls.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.310
She looked into the mirror and smiled shyly.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.549
With her short curls, she looked almost like a cheerful little schoolboy!

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:39.146
She wondered what Jim would say when he saw her.

00:03:39.996 --> 00:03:44.625
At seven o’clock the coffee was steaming on the stove, and supper was ready to cook.

00:03:44.875 --> 00:03:46.283
Jim was never late.

00:03:46.533 --> 00:03:55.088
Della folded the shining platinum watch chain in her hand and sat near the parlor door, listening to the sound of his boots coming up the stairs.

00:03:55.338 --> 00:03:59.711
“Please, dear Lord,” she whispered softly, “make him think I am still pretty.”

00:04:00.561 --> 00:04:03.484
The door opened, and Jim stepped in out of the cold.

00:04:03.734 --> 00:04:12.993
He looked thin and tired in his worn brown overcoat, holding his hat in his ungloved hands and carrying a small paper-wrapped package under his arm.

00:04:13.243 --> 00:04:19.600
Then Jim stopped still by the door, his eyes wide with quiet wonder as he looked at Della’s short curls.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:24.885
“You have cut off your hair?” asked Jim softly, as if he could hardly believe his eyes.

00:04:25.735 --> 00:04:28.529
Della ran across the room and took Jim’s hands.

00:04:28.779 --> 00:04:31.275
“Jim, darling, don’t look at me that way!

00:04:31.525 --> 00:04:37.627
I had my hair cut off and sold it because I couldn’t have lived through Christmas without giving you a present.

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:46.218
It’ll grow out again — and oh, Jim, wait till you see what I got for you!” At that, a warm, gentle smile spread across Jim’s face.

00:04:46.468 --> 00:04:54.575
He wrapped his arms around his Della and hugged her close, telling her that no haircut in the world could ever make him love his girl any less.

00:04:54.825 --> 00:04:59.241
Then he took the little paper-wrapped package from his coat and laid it on the table.

00:05:00.091 --> 00:05:06.149
Della untied the string and unfolded the white paper — and then she gave a little gasp of pure joy!

00:05:06.399 --> 00:05:16.831
Resting inside were The Combs: the set of three tortoise-shell hair combs with sparkling jeweled rims that she had admired for months in a shop window on Broadway.

00:05:17.081 --> 00:05:20.943
They were the exact warm shade to wear in her vanished chestnut hair.

00:05:21.193 --> 00:05:25.097
Della hugged them to her heart and looked up at Jim with shining eyes.

00:05:25.347 --> 00:05:27.075
“Oh, they are so beautiful!

00:05:27.325 --> 00:05:29.650
And my hair grows awfully fast, Jim!”

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:39.204
Then Della jumped up and held out her own gift in her open palm, where the braided platinum chain flashed like pale silver in the lamplight.

00:05:39.454 --> 00:05:43.977
She asked Jim for his gold watch so they could see how handsome it looked together.

00:05:44.227 --> 00:05:51.821
Instead of reaching into his pocket, Jim sat down on the floral settee, leaned back, and smiled the warmest smile of all.

00:05:52.071 --> 00:05:55.698
“Dell, let’s put our Christmas presents away and keep them awhile.

00:05:55.948 --> 00:05:58.188
They’re too nice to use just at present.

00:05:58.438 --> 00:06:01.723
I sold the gold watch to get the money to buy your combs.”

00:06:02.573 --> 00:06:10.531
Long ago, the Magi — the three wonderfully wise men — traveled beneath a bright star to bring the very first Christmas gifts.

00:06:10.781 --> 00:06:16.349
Some might say that Della and Jim were foolish to trade away the two greatest treasures of their house.

00:06:16.599 --> 00:06:26.604
Yet as they sat hand in hand in their warm little parlor while the snow fell outside, they had given each other something far greater than gold or jewels: their whole hearts.

00:06:26.854 --> 00:06:31.867
And of all who give and receive gifts anywhere in the world, such as they are the wisest.

00:06:32.117 --> 00:06:33.845
Everywhere they are the wisest.

00:06:34.095 --> 00:06:35.311
They are the Magi.
